00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.560
Let's start with a quick
Apigee Edge overview.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.140
This is our design
philosophy at Apigee.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.550
This challenges traditional
approaches of API management.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.250
Traditionally, we think
about this from an exposure

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.660
point of view--

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:03.410
they expose a set of services
and publish an API catalog,

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:04.970
and they think the job is done.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:06.650
Well, not really.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.590
Thinking about it from
an end user point of view

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:13.880
and working backwards from
there leads to better results.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.150
Realizing the importance of
each actor on this chain,

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.380
and providing them with
enhanced experiences,

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.010
becomes the key for a
successful API program.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:29.690
This outside-in approach is the
underlying principle at Apigee,

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:34.760
using which we have shown great
success to many of our clients.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.760
Having said that,
let's change the lens

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.820
and look at this pattern from
a security point of view.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.520
Indeed, the pattern
covers a lot of ground,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.830
which opens up a
need for a larger

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.230
set of security controls.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.930
Each actor on this
chain has a unique set

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.970
of security requirements.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.310
Let us start from left
to right, following

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:56.750
the outside-in approach.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.600
As we publish our
APIs for end users,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.390
how can we protect
these APIs from hackers

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.760
who are trying to
bring our systems down,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.550
and from data scrapers
and bad bots who

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.100
wants to steal our data?

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.950
Thereby leading to the
next question-- how can

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.860
I know that incoming calls
are from legit users,

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.710
and do they really
have needed permissions

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:21.110
to access these APIs?

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:23.330
An enterprise can
have many applications

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.490
that access your APIs--

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.700
how can I uniquely identify
these applications?

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.490
More importantly, as we are
looking at it from a security

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:33.740
and governance
perspective, how can I

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.660
ensure that the
decommissioned apps

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.390
can no longer access this API?

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.730
Moving further in towards
the right of the chain

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.515
comes the app developer.

00:02:42.515 --> 00:02:47.700
App developer uses our APIs
to create great applications.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.540
Now, how can we onboard
these app developers?

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.160
Remember, these
app developers can

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.080
be internal employees,
third party partners,

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.380
and any developer
on the internet.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.420
How can we register
him and provide him

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.540
with needed access
without much friction?

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.940
Moving towards the
APIs and API teams,

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.710
how do we ensure that the
API developers are developing

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:14.630
APIs that are secure and are not
opening up any vulnerability?

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:19.380
Are they adhering to
enterprise security standards?

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.150
Finally, on the backend, how
do we authenticate backend?

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.150
How do we pass in the
originating user's information

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.640
to the backend services?

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.450
How do we secure data on
wire and data at rest?

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.500
Yeah, the list is long.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.630
We need an API platform that
provides effective solution

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.190
for all these questions.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:44.030
Apigee, as a comprehensive
API platform,

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:47.930
provides standards-based
adaptive security controls,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.380
addressing those diverse set
of security requirements.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.970
Apigee protects against hackers
and data scrapers leveraging

00:03:55.970 --> 00:04:00.110
the Apigee Sense module, which
is a behavior-based threat

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:01.130
protection module.

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:04.760
Apigee protects our APIs against
threatening content attacks

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.910
like SQL injections,
cross-site scripting attacks,

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:12.260
leveraging the signature-based
type protection policy.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.260
Apigee effectively
controls spikes in traffic,

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:19.110
thereby protecting our backend
services from going down.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:23.640
Apigee identifies the
applications based on API keys

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.000
and authenticates end user
leveraging the industry

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.460
standard OAuth protocol.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.580
After identification
and authentication

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.780
comes the challenge
of API authorization.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:39.830
Apigee introduces this
concept of products.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:42.620
API products help
you slice and dice

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:47.530
your APIs in ways that
makes business sense.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:51.580
These products, in turn, act
as cross-grain authorization

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.830
enforcers.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:59.020
For final grain authorization,
Apigee leverages OAuth scopes.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:02.260
Apart from that, products
have assigned quotas

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:05.110
for enforcing usage boundaries.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:08.260
And last but not the
least, logging capabilities

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:13.600
for auditability, thus covering
identification, authentication,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.260
authorization, and auditability
aspects of security.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:22.280
Apigee works with your identity
and access management products,

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:27.290
like SideWinders, Ping
[INAUDIBLE] et cetera,

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:31.490
for providing single sign-on
and self-service experiences

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:33.410
for your app developers.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:38.330
Apigee supports OpenId Connect,
JWT, and SAML protocols

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:42.860
to work with your partners
in a federated fashion.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:46.030
Apigee provides global
policies and flow hooks

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:49.600
for abstracting
development and enforcement

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.280
of security policies from
business API developers.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:58.840
Apigee provides developer portal
as well as management APIs

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.270
for provisioning and
decommissioning apps

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:03.160
and application developers.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.520
Apigee secures data on
wire and in-transit,

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.850
leveraging one-way
and two-way SSL.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:12.730
Apigee can also store your
sensitive configuration

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.790
elements in an
encrypted fashion,

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:16.570
thereby providing
confidentiality

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:19.040
of your purchaser data.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.660
This is a high level overview
of Apigee security landscape.

