00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.650
Today, I'm going to
showcase how Apigee Edge

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:42.760
can be configured to protect
against threat protection.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:45.760
So the problem is when
a malicious client

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.820
can send malformed requests
to your backend services,

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.460
as showcased in
my diagram below.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:55.120
This includes all components
that make up the HTTP request

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.340
or message from those
clients, whether it

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:02.500
be TLS certificates, request
headers, the payload, or even

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.190
the query string fields
and/or parameters.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.430
And our client can be anything.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:10.550
It can be a browser,
mobile device, a server,

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:12.370
or even a IoT device.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:15.910
The API proxy layer should
detect, block, and report

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:19.150
any malformed requests that
are received from those clients

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:20.590
before the requests
are processed

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.180
on the backend systems.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.600
Too often, this is an oversight
and is not implemented, or not

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.030
even a priority when
developers are building out

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.080
backend systems.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:32.830
OWASP, which is the Open Web
Application Security Project,

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:36.100
is an open community that
is dedicated to enabling

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.580
organizations to implement web
API security best practices.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:43.330
OWASP publishes a top 10 list
of the most critical security

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:45.640
risks to web
applications and APIs.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.410
Their latest
publication last year

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:51.010
labeled injection flaws as
the number one security risk.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.920
In this demo, we'll cover
two of those topics.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.730
Input validation, which--
user-supplied data is not

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.890
validated, filtered,
or sanitized

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.540
before the application
receives it.

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:05.380
It should only be applied to
both syntactical and semantic

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:06.130
levels--

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:10.540
that's structured fields
and/or in value ranges, i.e.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:14.470
only permit valid payloads
or depths and lengths.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:20.230
Key, value is one depth,
whereas key 2, key 3

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:22.030
would be multiple depths.

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:25.360
And also, validate query
parameter fields, i.e.,

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:28.510
for this demo, I'm only going
to validate the limit, name,

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:30.250
and brewery key fields.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:32.530
Another example
of injection flaws

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:35.260
is SQL code injection, i.e.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:40.450
when dynamic queries are calls
without any contextual where

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:43.809
escaping are used to
directly hit the interpreter

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:46.630
or the application
context, i.e.,

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:50.980
let's search on the filter
for parameter values where

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:54.070
name='DELETE from resource.'

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:58.360
This can be applied in addition
to not just relational,

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.580
but also NoSQL queries.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:06.220
And a lot of times, it can
be implemented on ORM levels,

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:08.410
or object relational mappings--

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:10.390
abstraction layers
from a database

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:14.360
that may have direct SQL
queries implemented as well.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.800
Now, in this diagram,
on the right-hand side,

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:22.060
there is a RESTful Beer service
that is returning valid JSON

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:23.590
data to client.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:27.730
We want to only allow good
requests from valid clients

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:32.920
and detect, block, and
report on the bad requests

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:34.510
from those malicious clients.

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:37.450
The goals are create
an API that protects

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:40.330
against those injection flaws
from the client requests,

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:44.020
payloads, and query parameters,
keep it as simple as possible,

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:47.020
make it reproducible
for any additional APIs,

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:49.540
and also be able to
report on the usage

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:51.610
of those malformed requests.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:53.230
Now let's get to the API.

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.633
Let's log into my
Apigee Edge account

00:09:55.633 --> 00:09:57.550
and take a look at the
proxy that I've already

00:09:57.550 --> 00:09:59.020
created for this demo.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:09.780
My proxy, my Beers proxy,
already has a deployment URL.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:14.310
You can see my FQDN here with
the endpoint of forward slash

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:15.880
v1/beer.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:20.400
Here's my target endpoint on
my backend Beer service called

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.970
v1/beer-backend.

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:25.400
Once-- let's take a look
at the actual policies

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:29.610
that we can run through
with this proxy.

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:32.070
You'll see that I have
a couple of policies

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:33.960
that are already defined here.

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:37.500
Out of the box,
Apigee Edge includes

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:40.800
a whole suite of palettes--
or a whole palette

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:44.890
of different policy
assertions [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:49.360
traffic management, security,
mediation for transformation,

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:51.203
as well as extendability.

00:10:51.203 --> 00:10:53.620
Today, we're just going to
focus on a couple of the threat

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.270
protections--

00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:58.660
JSON Threat Protection
for input and validation,

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.840
as well as SQL Code
Protection and Parameter

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.870
Validation for injection flaws.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.810
So on my Details flow,
I've already went ahead

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:11.410
and created the JSON Threat
Protection, a parameter

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:14.680
validation leveraging
a JavaScript callout.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:17.710
I also have a custom fault
detection for that JavaScript

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:23.030
Parameter Validation, and
then also SQL Code Protection.

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:27.020
In a real world
production API proxy,

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:29.170
you would also have
some AuthN-AuthZ

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:31.990
to identify the client,
as well as any IP

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:36.260
tables or
restriction-based rules

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:39.020
before any of these
additional policies.

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:43.970
Let's take a look at JSON Threat
Protection as our first policy.

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:47.420
We'll see there's a couple
different parameters

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:49.610
or variables that
are defined here.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.800
Our ArrayElementCount
is the first one.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:56.540
That means that this JSON
threat structure will not

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.220
include any arrays.

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:00.680
It will just actually
be a key value.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.390
And the depth of
those values will just

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:05.360
be 1, so there won't
be any nested depth

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:09.350
or nested key value objects
within the JSON structure.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:13.490
We want to keep the number of
keys or the number of entries

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:16.580
