being captured.

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:20.530
So first things first,
let's try that POST again.

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:26.200
This time, I'm going to
do a nested structure

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:30.200
as my value instead of
just a standard name.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:38.840
Oh, immediately I got a 400 back
from my Apigee Edge API proxy.

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:42.270
You'll see that I have a
custom faultstring or fault

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:46.440
message that is actually
identifying the policy itself,

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:49.550
which is JSONThreatProtection,
and more details of "exceeded

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:51.620
the container depth at line 1."

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:55.920
And we look at our trace
session and there's that 400.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:58.670
And if you walk
through the trace path,

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:01.250
you'll see that that
JSONThreatProtection

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:02.840
was triggered right here.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:04.430
Excellent.

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:08.570
Now, what if I have a
random array of strings

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:11.640
that are over 255 characters?

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:15.290
Let's try that same POST
method, this time with--

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:25.000
there it is, a 400
immediately back

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:28.780
from Apigee Edge
in my API proxy,

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:32.490
and you'll see the same
policy was triggered,

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:34.500
JSONThreatProtection,
with a different value,

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:35.430
though, this time--

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:41.250
"exceeded string value
length at line 1."

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:43.100
Likewise, there it is--

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:46.450
I got the error right here
on the JSONThreatProtection.

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:49.170
Now, each time that
actually occurs,

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:51.990
I'm actually doing some
additional logging.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:53.550
You'll see these
other policies here

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:56.220
that I'll get into where I'm
going to capture those error

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:58.560
codes and we'll be able
to report them later.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:03.720
But this showcases that with
a simple drag and drop policy,

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.840
you can configure and have
those parameters you'd

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:11.340
find to [INAUDIBLE] against
JSON threats or within the key

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:14.520
as well as the value in
the structure itself.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:16.370
Now, for the next
example, let's take

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:20.240
a look at SQL code
injection flaws,

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:25.320
as well as URL
parameter query strings.

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:33.130
So let's go back to my policies
on my detail policy flow.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:36.160
Now, I have a couple
of additional policies

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:39.010
out next to my JSON
Threat Protection.

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:41.260
The first one is
Parameter Validation,

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:42.910
and for Parameter
Validation, I'm

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:45.270
actually going to leverage
our JavaScript callout

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:48.460
feature where I can write
custom JavaScript and custom

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:52.060
logic within Apigee Edge
to manipulate the message

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:55.720
or session, as well as
define custom variables

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:59.020
from the requests coming in.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:01.570
So within my Paramter
Validation JavaScript,

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:05.470
I'm actually going to create
a whiteList of parameters

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:09.280
or query strings that are only
valid for my backend client.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:14.859
In this case, I only want to
allow the query string name,

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:19.630
brewery, and limit to be
able to be passed back

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:23.530
to my backend resource.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:26.410
I set a custom
faultResponse message

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:31.960
here so that I can capture it if
this ParameterValidation threat

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:33.940
protection is triggered,
and I'm actually

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:38.185
going to go and iterate
over the request parameters.

00:17:38.185 --> 00:17:41.890
I'll iterate over
the whiteList to see

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:43.720
if any of those
request parameters

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:45.820
meet one of those
variables in the whiteList,

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:48.880
and then if I'll flag one--

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.380
If they're true, they're good.

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:54.010
If not, I'll flag them as
false and I'll actually

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:58.270
set a context variable called
parameterValidation.fault

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:00.580
to set a custom fault
message so that I

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:02.950
can report on that accordingly.

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:06.860
Once that custom fault message
is set, I'll actually raise--

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:11.760
I'll set a custom parameter
validation fault message here

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:14.060
to be able to report
on that accordingly.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:16.127
Let's go back to
my trace session.

00:18:16.127 --> 00:18:16.960
We're still running.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:17.460
Great.

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:24.952
Let's simulate a successful call
again with those query strings.

00:18:24.952 --> 00:18:27.160
We'll see there's that best
beer variable that I just

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:28.760
created earlier.

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:31.450
Now, if I change my
query parameter--

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:36.250
in addition to limit and name,
if I add, let's say, test=123,

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:42.310
which was not a valid parameter,
immediately I get a 400 back

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:46.390
from my Apigee Edge API proxy.

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:48.550
And here's a
different definition

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:51.730
or a different policy--

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:53.290
"ParameterValidation.

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:55.397
Execution failed,
reason-- 'test'

00:18:55.397 --> 00:18:56.480
is not a valid parameter."

00:18:56.480 --> 00:18:57.512
That is true.

00:18:57.512 --> 00:18:59.470
That wasn't one of those
whitelisted parameters

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:02.950
that I defined in my
JavaScript callout.

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:07.960
And you'll see right here that
the JSONThreatProtection was

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:12.850
not triggered, but the
actual ParameterValidation

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:13.720
went through.

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.150
It iterated over
those three values,

00:19:16.150 --> 00:19:20.195
and then I set this fault
that this was triggered,

00:19:20.195 --> 00:19:22.570
and then I caught that fault
and I raised the fault based

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:26.960
off my ParameterValidation.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.880
Just as like that, you
can have whitelisted

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:31.740
parameters that
you can call out,

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:36.030
in addition to JSON Threat
Protection for injection flaws.

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:40.655
Now, what if the client is going
to send those malformed SQL

00:19:40.655 --> 00:19:41.880
code?

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:49.050
Let's go back to my policy,
and here is SQL code.

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.900
This is actually a SQL
Code Protection policy

00:19:51.900 --> 00:20:00.060
where I'm allowing Apigee
Edge to identify the invalid

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:02.970
SQL that is being
sent by the values

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:05.610
of those query
parameters with a name.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:08.730
So whether it be the query,
the header, or the form,

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:12.390
I am looking-- at I
have a set regex which

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:16.530
was defined by OWASP where
delete, execute, drop, insert,

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:20.790
shut down, or update, or any of
those standard relational SQL

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:25.260
or NoSQL queries can be
captured and this policy

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:27.250
can be triggered.

00:20:27.250 --> 00:20:28.693
So let's take another example.

00:20:28.693 --> 00:20:30.110
Let's go back to
my trace session.

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:37.130
So if I do a get, this
time, let's change

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:46.170
name to be DELETE from brewery.

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:48.880
Let's try that.

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:50.800
Oh, just like that.

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:56.050
Once again, I got another 400
back from my Apigee Edge API

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:57.040
proxy.

00:20:57.040 --> 00:21:01.000
And you'll see this time, it's
regular expression detected,

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:04.150
and actually, am identifying
DELETE from brewery

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:09.970
was invalid parameter,
or invalid value

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:11.480
for that parameter.

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:13.740
I can go back to
my trace session.

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:15.880
Let's skip over
here, and you'll see

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:21.340
that the SQLCodeProtection was
actually triggered this time.

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:25.330
And just like that, I just--
in those three policies,

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:29.500
I was able to do
input validation

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:33.070
through nested JSON
and JSON string length,

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:36.970
as well as injection flaws from
query parameters and SQL code

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:38.770
injection failures.

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:43.270
Now, I did mention that
I needed to capture--

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:48.130
I had some additional policies
to capture those analytics,

00:21:48.130 --> 00:21:49.990
because the idea
of the API proxy

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:54.970
is not just to detect and
block those threat protections,

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:57.490
but also report on them, too.

00:21:57.490 --> 00:22:05.020
So I have these additional
FaultResponse flows right down

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:11.050
here where I'm going to extract
the error codes that are coming

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:12.580
back from this file response.

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:17.320
And I'm going to try and
capture that error code variable

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:20.350
so that I can report on them
through our Apigee Edge's

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:21.640
analytics.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:24.340
Now, that value right
here is actually

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:30.250
just a key value error
code with the value

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:32.600
here is steps.regexprotection.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:34.920
And if I look at
the other one, it

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:37.420
is steps.parametervalidation.

00:22:37.420 --> 00:22:41.050
So within this policy, I'm
actually doing a JSONPath

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:45.100
on that variable or on that
detail error code variable,

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:48.310
and then saving
that as a context

00:22:48.310 --> 00:22:51.040
variable called FaultErrorCode.

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:54.640
That FaultErrorCode
is now going to be

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:58.360
leveraged through
our FaultCollector.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.280
And this FaultCollector
is basically

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:04.210
a statistics collector that
will capture that variable

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:07.180
and set it as a new value
within our analytics

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:08.990
so that I can report on it.

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:11.180
So that FaultErrorCode
variable will actually

00:23:11.180 --> 00:23:16.320
be set as fault_error_code
within my analytics.

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:18.740
I went ahead and
created a report.

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:21.570
So let's see if
that is available.

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:26.010
It reports on those
error codes based off

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:28.480
of the sum of traffic.

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:31.760
So if I go into this report--

00:23:31.760 --> 00:23:33.020
oh, there it is.

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:37.480
You'll see that I am
capturing each one of them.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.710
Looks like there's the
threatprotection, here's

00:23:39.710 --> 00:23:42.830
the parametervalidation,
here's the regexprotection.

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:44.690
Looks like I have a
couple ones that are not

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:47.210
set that I'll have to go back
and clean up to make sure

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:50.660
that they're not being
sent anonymously.

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:54.500
But just like that,
you're able to protect

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:58.850
within Apigee Edge for threat
protection injection flaws

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:00.650
as well as input
validation, and then

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:04.362
report on them within
our analytics platform.

00:24:04.362 --> 00:24:07.023
And that concludes this demo.

00:24:07.023 --> 00:24:09.690
Thanks you very much, and let me
know if you have any questions.

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.590
NASEER MOHAMMAD: Thank you for
really, really wonderful demo

00:24:12.590 --> 00:24:13.090
there.

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:17.610
All right, so we'll take a pause
here for question and answers.

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:20.810
So guys, please do
ask your questions.

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:24.600
I'll be pouring through
the Q&amp;A channel here.

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:28.280
So the first question in
here goes as follows--

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:32.960
"Can Apigee be configured
to detect false positives

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:36.830
while performing
signature-based threats?

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:40.590
Using our current [INAUDIBLE]
and cloud-based firewall,

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:44.780
we can block the calls that
have threatening signatures,

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:48.680
but they do not tell us
why the calls were blocked.

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:51.680
Can Apigee provide
additional information

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:53.750
on why the request was blocked?"

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:56.210
So that's the question.

00:24:56.210 --> 00:25:01.730
And so we have seen this
before from many of our clients

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:06.770
as well who are using
[INAUDIBLE] or any firewall

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:09.110
in the network side.

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:11.960
When it comes to threatening
content protection,

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:15.590
it is very important for us
to get the signatures right.

00:25:15.590 --> 00:25:18.020
At times, what
happens is you might

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:21.170
have a signature or a
[INAUDIBLE] expression that

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:27.530
corresponds to a signature, and
it might also capture or mark

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:32.190
the requests that are not
really threatening content,

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:34.190
because they do not really
have any content that

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:37.290
was threatening but
was a false positive.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:41.240
So yes, in Apigee,
you can configure

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:47.000
your threatening content
policies to report back

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:52.670
onto why the call was blocked,
thereby your developers

00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:56.090
can go back and can see
why the call was blocked,

00:25:56.090 --> 00:26:00.470
or it can go to your analytics
consoles or your logging

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:04.940
systems whereby the operations
folks can go and see

00:26:04.940 --> 00:26:07.530
why a particular transaction
was [? designed. ?]

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:09.680
Hope that answers that question.

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:11.810
So the next question
I have here is,

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:16.460
can Apigee be integrated with
cloud-based identity solutions

00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:19.060
like Okta?

00:26:19.060 --> 00:26:22.660
Yes, we have many
times we're using Okta

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:27.490
and are also using Apigee
for API management.

00:26:27.490 --> 00:26:30.430
The general answer--
like yes, the answer

00:26:30.430 --> 00:26:34.240
is yes for Okta, but in
general, for any cloud identity

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:39.700
solution, as long as the cloud
identity provider supports

00:26:39.700 --> 00:26:44.480
a SAML protocol or
OpenId Connect protocol,

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:46.420
we can work with them.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:49.780
We have also worked with some of
the cloud providers that really

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:52.510
do not support a fully
OpenId Connect protocol,

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:57.310
but they have OAuth
implementation, which

00:26:57.310 --> 00:26:58.910
we can integrate with as well.

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:01.210
So the short answer
is yes, we support

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:04.160
Okta, the cloud-based
identity solution.

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:05.980
So there's another
question here--

00:27:05.980 --> 00:27:09.460
how can I configure
behavior-based threat

00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:11.740
protection modules?

00:27:11.740 --> 00:27:14.770
Like, where can I
set the upper limits

00:27:14.770 --> 00:27:19.900
and lower limits of, for
example, a brute force attack?

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:21.280
All right, so
that's interesting.

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:23.920
So what we have seen
in this demo today

00:27:23.920 --> 00:27:26.540
is signature-based
threat protection, right?

00:27:26.540 --> 00:27:29.740
So there's also a
complementary aspect

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:31.330
of signature-based
protection, which

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:33.790
is also known as [INAUDIBLE]
behavior-based threat

00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:35.260
protection.

00:27:35.260 --> 00:27:37.930
Apigee has a product
called Apigee Sense that

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:43.450
does provide those capabilities,
and Apigee Sense provides--

00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:46.720
basically, what
it provides you--

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:49.000
settings that are based
on convention, based

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:53.620
on our experiences, as to
how you mark a certain attack

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:55.930
as a brute force attack
or a password guesser

00:27:55.930 --> 00:27:57.040
attack [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:57.040 --> 00:28:00.340
And then out of those
settings, we give full control

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:05.470
to our customers to come and
fine tune those settings based

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:06.770
on their needs.

00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:11.150
So yes, Apigee does provide
you configuration capabilities

00:28:11.150 --> 00:28:13.600
to configure your adaptive
behavior-based detection

00:28:13.600 --> 00:28:16.900
algorithms based on your inputs.

00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:20.240
So I hope that
answers that question.

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:21.880
CHRIS PAGE: OK,
let me check on one

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:23.550
of these other next questions.

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:28.450
Does SQL Code Protection only
apply to relational databases?

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:31.330
Now that's-- you
know, yes and no.

00:28:31.330 --> 00:28:34.720
There's a common misconception
that if developers are not

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:37.500
writing SQL queries for
their SQL databases,

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:40.630
that SQL code injection
is really not applicable.

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:43.060
There are definitely
well-known SQL vulnerabilities

00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:45.700
via associated arrays
and where clauses.

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:50.200
You can definitely check out on
some of the black hat and OWASP

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:50.830
websites.

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:55.750
But there's some documentation
for testing against SQL code

00:28:55.750 --> 00:28:57.372
on NoSQL data stores.

00:28:57.372 --> 00:28:58.580
NASEER MOHAMMAD: Sounds good.

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:00.550
So the next question
that is in here

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:07.060
is should an ORM database
framework library handle

00:29:07.060 --> 00:29:09.580
all SQL injection protection?

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:11.560
That's a good question.

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:13.790
So the best practice
for security

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:15.790
is to block the
threatening content

00:29:15.790 --> 00:29:21.670
before it comes to
your folder [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:21.670 --> 00:29:22.810
on the database side.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:25.630
So for example, let's
say your ORM basically

00:29:25.630 --> 00:29:28.530
is searching your internet
right where the data is.

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:33.190
So the fact that the
threatening content vector

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:38.040
reached and managed to reach at
that level in itself is a risk.

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:41.530
So we definitely want to
have our ORM databases

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:45.340
to implement SQL
injection protections,

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:50.260
but it should be handled on the
edge of the API gateway layers

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:53.710
so that we provide at the
first level of defense, which

00:29:53.710 --> 00:29:57.310
is our API gateway,
making sure that there's

00:29:57.310 --> 00:30:00.670
no threatening vectors or
[INAUDIBLE] signatures that

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:02.990
are coming in and
protected over there.

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:08.620
So the answer is yes, the
protection should be on the ORM

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:09.560
as well.

00:30:09.560 --> 00:30:12.430
But it is always best
practice to protect the calls

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.270
at the edge layers.

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:15.580
So I'll take up the second--

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:17.350
the next question as well here.

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:21.100
Can Apigee prevent
malicious requests,

00:30:21.100 --> 00:30:24.070
or can it only detect?

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:28.390
If it can prevent, can these
prevented malicious calls

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:31.132
be reported to our
incident management teams?

00:30:31.132 --> 00:30:32.090
That's a good question.

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:35.530
So yes, Apigee is
both detection--

00:30:35.530 --> 00:30:38.590
works both as a detection
and prevention mechanisms.

00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:41.950
So it has protective controls
and it has preventive controls

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:42.720
as well.

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:46.420
Apigee can very well, like Chris
showed in the demonstration,

00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:50.200
can detect and prevent and
block the call right there.

00:30:50.200 --> 00:30:54.582
If you want, Apigee can also
detect and record the call.

00:30:54.582 --> 00:30:56.290
I think that that's
the question-- that's

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:58.240
where the question was
leading towards, right?

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:04.360
So it can definitely detect and
notify an incident management

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:06.250
team to take
appropriate actions.

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:08.560
Obviously, the
preferred approach

00:31:08.560 --> 00:31:13.240
is to prevent and detect in
Apigee and after detection,

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:16.690
block the call and then notify
to the incident management team

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:19.110
so that the incident
management team can

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:21.060
take the appropriate
actions on that.

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:23.310
Hope that answers that question.

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:25.690
Chris, you want to
go for the next one?

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:26.620
CHRIS PAGE: Sure.

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:28.120
Do you-- here's the question.

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:31.000
Do you have to have
customized SQL injection

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:34.990
regex for threat protection,
or something universal which

00:31:34.990 --> 00:31:36.970
can be used?

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:43.200
So there's a wide range
of threat protections

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:46.690
that can be identified
from the communities,

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:51.270
and there isn't one unique
regex that would solve all.

00:31:51.270 --> 00:31:55.830
So you would basically define
those unique regex patterns

00:31:55.830 --> 00:31:57.505
based off of that
particular threat.

00:32:00.210 --> 00:32:04.600
This next one-- is there
a list of out of the box

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:06.670
security policies that
you would recommend

00:32:06.670 --> 00:32:09.850
to use as a starting
point for most APIs?

00:32:09.850 --> 00:32:12.520
Now, that's a good question.

00:32:12.520 --> 00:32:14.050
A lot of the times,
we hear that--

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:16.480
what are those best practices--

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:19.330
from our customers, of what
security policies should

00:32:19.330 --> 00:32:22.300
be applied to those APIs.

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:27.060
And it's really going to
depend what type of API it is.

00:32:27.060 --> 00:32:29.020
Is it a SOAP-based service?

00:32:29.020 --> 00:32:31.660
Is it a RESTful service?

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:35.380
You know, is it a public-facing,
is it an internal API?

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:40.210
We have a lot of those
protections we would definitely

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:43.900
implement for those APIs
right out of the box.

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:46.360
We also have a couple of other
policies within our palette

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:50.230
that we didn't go into
depth within this tutorial

00:32:50.230 --> 00:32:51.640
or this webinar.

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:58.360
Something like verifying client
quota or rate limiting or spike

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:01.185
arrests would also be
applied as best practices.

00:33:01.185 --> 00:33:02.560
And you can find
out more of them

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:05.180
through our community and our
doc sites within the Resources

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:05.680
tab.

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:07.430
NASEER MOHAMMAD: I
have two questions here

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:09.550
which are related.

00:33:09.550 --> 00:33:13.270
How do you validate the
input request parameters

00:33:13.270 --> 00:33:14.260
with the data types?

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:18.010
Example, if I want
to send H paramaters,

00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:20.110
I would not like to take
a [INAUDIBLE] input.

00:33:20.110 --> 00:33:24.920
So that question and
the question about also,

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:29.660
can you validate the URL
parameters and body payloads

00:33:29.660 --> 00:33:32.650
validating against
a JSON schema?

00:33:32.650 --> 00:33:36.590
So it also-- it's a data
validation question.

00:33:36.590 --> 00:33:41.660
Yes, you can validate your
input incoming request, again,

00:33:41.660 --> 00:33:45.290
like JSON schema or XML schema,
and the schema validation

00:33:45.290 --> 00:33:49.490
is definitely very good step
for you to validate the data.

00:33:49.490 --> 00:33:51.560
You can definitely do that.

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:56.270
What you can also do is, because
we are focused on threatening

00:33:56.270 --> 00:33:59.660
content protection,
we can also validate

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:02.870
the complexity of the JSON.

00:34:02.870 --> 00:34:06.860
There are a few attacks that
are sophisticated in the nature

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:11.840
that they try to emulate
a denial of service attack

00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:16.080
by sending a huge JSON
payload or an XML payload that

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:18.690
your backends cannot handle.

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:22.130
So Apigee can definitely--

00:34:22.130 --> 00:34:25.190
rapidly handles
complexity policy

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:27.219
wherein you can define
your complexity levels,

00:34:27.219 --> 00:34:32.929
saying that the allowed max
cap on a number of elements

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:36.730
in a JSON payload
is less than 50.

00:34:36.730 --> 00:34:39.810
So anyone who's trying
to send more than that,

00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:44.940
we will mark that as a threat
so that we can do that.

00:34:44.940 --> 00:34:47.420
So the answer is
yes, data validation

00:34:47.420 --> 00:34:51.530
can be done via schema, XML
schemas and JSON schemas.

00:34:51.530 --> 00:34:56.195
You can actually go find way and
then you pick the query params

00:34:56.195 --> 00:34:59.300
or should it be headers
and also validate

00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:02.000
the data using
[INAUDIBLE] filters.

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:04.640
And then who can take that
to the next level, where

00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:06.320
you're also adding
the complexity

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:08.040
filters on top of it.

00:35:08.040 --> 00:35:09.830
Hope that answers that question.

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:11.413
CHRIS PAGE: These
